framecmp -- is the decoded picture right?
=========================================

`framecmp.py` scores a framestore dump against `tools/mpeg2dec`, the reference
C decoder. It works on a dump from the Icarus simulation and on a dump of DDR
from the PolarFire board, using the same code for both.

This replaces the ad-hoc "MAD of the framestore ppm against mpeg2dec" that was
tried during Fase 7a and gave meaningless numbers (the known-good simulation
scored MAD ~94, the hardware ~121, so the method was wrong, not the hardware).
What that method got wrong, and this one gets right:

  - framestore pixels are **signed, offset by -128** (`motcomp_recon.v`), not
    unsigned. Reading them unsigned shifts every pixel by 128, which on its own
    produces a MAD near 128 -- essentially the whole of the old number.
  - the leftmost pixel of a 64-bit word is bits [63:56], so in a little-endian
    dump the eight pixels of a word are **reversed**.
  - there is no fixed mapping from frame buffer to displayed frame, so every
    buffer is scored against every reference frame and the best match is
    reported. A correct decode gives one near-zero score per buffer.
  - the reference's decoded output is `frame_NN_out_`, not `frame_NN_aux_`.
  - streams must be cut on a picture boundary (`framecmp.py cut`), otherwise
    the hardware writes a partial picture over a frame buffer the reference
    never produced a frame for.

How to use it
-------------

Build the reference decoder once:

    cd tools/mpeg2dec && make

Cut a test stream to a whole number of pictures and decode it with the
reference:

    python3 tools/framecmp/framecmp.py cut \
        tools/streams/ccett/mcp10ccett/mcp10ccett.bits 4 -o /tmp/s.bits
    python3 tools/framecmp/framecmp.py ref /tmp/s.bits -o /tmp/ref

Simulation: `bench/iverilog` writes `fs_NNNN.bin` + `fs_NNNN.txt` once per
completed picture (see `DUMP_FRAMESTORE_RAW` at the top of
`bench/iverilog/mem_ctl.v`). Point the same stream at the testbench, then:

    python3 tools/framecmp/framecmp.py compare bench/iverilog/fs_0003.bin /tmp/ref

Hardware: `webserver/capture_framestore.py` on the board (firmware_development
branch) pushes the stream and dumps the 12 MiB framestore region of
`/dev/udmabuf-ddr-nc0` in exactly the same format. Copy the `.bin` and `.txt`
to the host and run the same `compare`.

Reading the output
------------------

    buffer    best reference frame      MAD(Y)   PSNR(Y)    peak   chroma MAD (U/V)
    0         frame_00_out_              0.000       inf       0   0.000 / 0.000

  - **MAD 0.000, peak 0** -- bit exact with the reference.
  - **MAD below ~0.02, peak 1** -- the two IDCTs disagree in the last bit, which
    IEEE 1180 allows. This is what a correct decode normally looks like.
  - **MAD of a few units with peak over 100** -- real damage. Extract the planes
    (`framecmp.py extract --pgm`) and look at where it is.
  - **MAD ~128** -- the dump is being read as unsigned. Check `--base-word` and
    the memory map.
  - **"never written"** -- the plane is one uniform value. Capture poisons the
    framestore first so this really does mean the decoder never wrote there.

`--fast-idct` lets the reference use its own 8-bit IDCT instead of the double
precision one, which tells you how much of a difference is just two conformant
IDCTs disagreeing.

`--memory-map` must match how `rtl/mpeg2/mem_codes.v` was built; the default
`MP_AT_HL` is what `mem_codes.v` currently selects.
